        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe overall shape is good and follows the established conventions here: the mapper `ignore` for `riskFlag` mirrors its sibling derived fields, and `isRisk`/`isOverSoftCapacity` follow the same private-predicate-per-derived-field pattern as `isBulkSignup`/`isApproachingCapacity` in the controller. Placing `isDisposableAdjacent` on `EmailDomainValidator` is a reasonable call too \u2014 it keeps all disposable-domain knowledge (and the `BLOCKED_DOMAINS` set) in one class rather than duplicating the blocklist. But a few structural cleanups would make this more idiomatic:\n\n1. **Duplicated domain extraction in `EmailDomainValidator`** \u2014 `isDisposableAdjacent` re-implements the exact email\u2192domain parsing that `isValid` already does: `lastIndexOf('@')`, the `at < 0 || at == email.length() - 1` boundary check, and `substring(at + 1).toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)`. This 4-line block now appears twice in the same small class. Extract a shared `private static String domainOf(String email)` (returning null when absent) and have both methods call it. That removes the duplication and keeps the two entry points thin.\n\n2. **Soft-capacity threshold `40` duplicated in the controller** \u2014 both `isApproachingCapacity` (`count >= 40 && count <= 49`) and the new `isOverSoftCapacity` (`count >= 40`) hard-code the soft-capacity boundary of 40 (and reference the hard limit of 50). The \"soft capacity\" concept now lives as a magic number in two predicates; a change to it must be made in both. Consider a shared named constant (e.g. `SOFT_CAPACITY = 40`) so the two related predicates express the same boundary once.\n\n3. **Minor idiom nit** \u2014 `DISPOSABLE_BASES` uses the fully-qualified `java.util.stream.Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet()` inline instead of a normal import, unlike the rest of the file's `import` style. Add the import for consistency.\n\nNone of these change behavior; they're about keeping the added code non-duplicated and consistent with the surrounding style."
